<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Phase 13 / audit §2.3. Makes "at most one active role per user per branch" a database
      invariant, and removes the hardcoded dev HQ UUID from the login path.

      ── Why the repair comes first ──

      Creating the unique index on a database that already carries duplicates fails, and a
      migration that fails on real data is worse than no migration: it blocks every subsequent
      changeset for everyone. So the duplicates are resolved first, deterministically, and only
      then is the index created. The index doubles as the assertion that the repair worked — if any
      duplicate survives, this changeset rolls back in one piece rather than half-applying, because
      Postgres DDL is transactional and Liquibase runs a changeset in one transaction.

      Retention rule, stated so the outcome is reproducible across environments rather than
      whatever the planner happened to scan first: for each (user_id, branch_id) holding more than
      one active row, keep the one with the greatest (updated_at, id) — i.e. the most recently
      touched assignment, ties broken by the greater id. The rest are SOFT-deactivated, never
      deleted; the row and its audit columns survive, and updated_at records when the repair
      touched it. auth_db has no audit table reachable from here, so the updated_at change is the
      only record of the repair, which is why nothing is hard-deleted.

      ── Why RLS is stood down for the duration ──

      user_branch_roles is FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY with
      USING (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true), '')::uuid).
      Liquibase runs on the application datasource (auth_user, NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS — see
      deploy/init/02-create-roles.sql), and FORCE means even the table owner is subject to the
      policy. With no tenant GUC set, that predicate is NULL for every row, so a repair UPDATE
      written naively would match nothing, report success, and leave the duplicates in place — and
      then the index creation would fail with a constraint violation nobody could explain. Setting
      a GUC is not an option either: the repair has to span every tenant.

      So RLS is dropped to NO FORCE for the owner, the repair runs, and FORCE is restored. It is
      narrower than DISABLE (the policy stays active for every non-owner role throughout) and it is
      inside the changeset's transaction, so a failure anywhere below leaves FORCE exactly as it
      was. If auth_user is not the table owner in some environment, the ALTER fails loudly here,
      which is the correct outcome — far better than a silent no-op repair.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-056-repair-duplicate-active-branch-roles" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

            UPDATE user_branch_roles t
               SET is_active = false,
                   updated_at = NOW()
             WHERE t.is_active
               AND EXISTS (
                   SELECT 1 FROM user_branch_roles keeper
                    WHERE keeper.is_active
                      AND keeper.user_id = t.user_id
                      AND keeper.branch_id = t.branch_id
                      AND (keeper.updated_at, keeper.id) &gt; (t.updated_at, t.id)
               );

            ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>

        <!--
          Fail with a sentence rather than with a constraint-violation stack trace. If the repair
          matched nothing it is almost certainly because it could not see the rows, and the operator
          needs to be told that, not left to infer it from index creation failing on the next line.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE remaining BIGINT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT COUNT(*) INTO remaining FROM (
                    SELECT user_id, branch_id FROM user_branch_roles
                     WHERE is_active
                     GROUP BY user_id, branch_id
                    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
                ) d;
                IF remaining > 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                        'Duplicate-active-role repair left % (user_id, branch_id) pair(s) with more than one active row. The UPDATE above saw no rows to fix, which usually means row-level security hid them: this migration must run as the owner of user_branch_roles. Do NOT create the unique index until this is resolved.', remaining;
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <!--
              Intentionally empty. The forward path deactivated rows; re-activating them would
              recreate exactly the duplicates the next run has to repair again, and there is no
              record of which rows were deactivated by this changeset rather than by an
              administrator. Rolling back the index (next changeset) is enough to restore the
              schema; the data repair is deliberately one-way.
            -->
            <sql>SELECT 1;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-056-one-active-role-per-branch-index" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <!--
          A PARTIAL unique index, not addUniqueConstraint: deactivated history rows must remain, so
          the constraint has to be restricted to is_active, and Liquibase's addUniqueConstraint
          cannot express a WHERE clause. Hence raw SQL with an explicit rollback.

          tenant_id is deliberately not part of the key. user_id is a UUID and globally unique, so
          (user_id, branch_id) already identifies the pair; including tenant_id would only widen the
          key and weaken the invariant if a row were ever written under the wrong tenant.
        -->
        <sql>
            CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uk_user_branch_roles_one_active
                ON user_branch_roles (user_id, branch_id)
             WHERE is_active;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS uk_user_branch_roles_one_active;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-056-add-is-primary" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><columnExists tableName="user_branch_roles" columnName="is_primary"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <!--
          Replaces PermissionResolver.HQ_BRANCH_ID, a hardcoded dev branch UUID that decided which
          branch every user in every tenant landed on at login. It is right for exactly one tenant
          on one developer's database and silently arbitrary everywhere else — and the provisioning
          saga does not even mark the first branch as HQ, so the constant matches nothing on a
          freshly provisioned tenant.
        -->
        <addColumn tableName="user_branch_roles">
            <column name="is_primary" type="BOOLEAN" defaultValueBoolean="false">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="user_branch_roles" columnName="is_primary"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-056-backfill-is-primary" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <!--
          One primary per user, chosen deterministically: the earliest-created active assignment,
          ties broken by the lower id. Same NO FORCE reasoning as the repair above — without it this
          UPDATE matches nothing and every existing user silently falls through to the "no primary"
          branch of selectDefaultBranch.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

            UPDATE user_branch_roles
               SET is_primary = true
             WHERE id IN (
                 SELECT DISTINCT ON (user_id) id
                   FROM user_branch_roles
                  WHERE is_active
                  ORDER BY user_id, created_at, id
             );

            ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
                UPDATE user_branch_roles SET is_primary = false;
                ALTER TABLE user_branch_roles FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-056-one-primary-per-user-index" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <!--
          At most one active primary per user. Without this the flag is advisory and
          selectDefaultBranch's "prefer the primary" degenerates to "prefer whichever primary the
          query happened to return first" — a non-deterministic default branch at login, which is
          the class of bug this whole changeset exists to remove.
        -->
        <sql>
            CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uk_user_branch_roles_one_primary
                ON user_branch_roles (user_id)
             WHERE is_active AND is_primary;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS uk_user_branch_roles_one_primary;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
